# authz-assertion-without-owner.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M130c): an authorization assertion in a
# test that never says who made the request — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF063 (checker, tflw
# M130b2 / D329, the pentest arc's Tier 2).
#
# `expect response has no authorization violations` is a **differential** oracle: it takes the
# request the last `api` step actually made, re-issues it under every *other* declared principal,
# and asks whether the owner's resources came back to somebody else. "Every other" is a set
# subtraction, and it needs a minuend. A test with no `as <session>` names no owner, so there is no
# identity to subtract and nothing the comparison is *against* — the assertion cannot be wrong,
# which is the one thing an authorization check must be able to be.
#
# **This is a lexical check, and that is the interesting part.** The owner is not discovered by
# running anything: it is read off the `test` header at check time, so the failure lands in the
# editor rather than after a suite has already sent cross-identity traffic. The interpreter carries
# the same rule as a backstop (an `action` reached from a test whose owner the checker could not
# see), but the backstop fires *before any probe is sent* — the two guards agree on the answer and
# differ only in when they can know it.
#
# **The hook case is the same defect wearing different syntax, and it is why this file has a second
# test.** A `before file` / `after file` hook runs in its own scope, isolated from every test in the
# file (SPEC §3.3), so it can never inherit an owner — not because nobody wrote one, but because the
# construct has nowhere to put one. The repair is different too: move the assertion into a test, or
# into a **bare `before`/`after`**, which runs once per test and shares that test's scope. Note that
# `before each` is not the spelling — a bare `before` *is* the per-test hook.
#
# **The neighbouring rule this must not be confused with**: an owner is required, and *every* session
# a test names is an owner. `test "…" as admin, shopper` sends both identities on the request, so
# both are owners and neither is probed. That is a smaller probe set, not a missing one, and it is
# not this code.
#
# **Not covered here:** the all-sessions-`privileged` refusal, which is the *other* way the probe set
# can come up empty and shares this code. It needs a config where every declared `session` carries
# `privileged`, and this project's own `tflw.config` deliberately marks none of them — the declared
# side of that pair lives in `tflw-acceptance/security/`. Refusal here, permission there, the same
# split D291 uses for TF060.
#
# This project's `tflw.config` also declares no `authorized target`, so TF060 fires on these tests
# too. That is correct and not incidental: permission to scan and having somebody to compare against
# are independent requirements, and a suite that has neither should hear about both.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/authz-assertion-without-owner.tflw

before file
  # Isolated scope by construction — this one can never be repaired by adding `as`.
  api GET /orders
  expect response has no authorization violations

test "an authorization assertion with nobody behind it"
  api GET /orders
  expect response has no authorization violations

test "a severity floor does not supply the missing owner"
  api GET /orders
  # The floor narrows which rules run. It has nothing to do with whose resources are being compared.
  check response has no critical authorization violations

test "the neighbours the rule must not touch" as shopper
  api GET /orders
  # An owner is declared, so these are ordinary assertions and the authz rule stays silent.
  expect status equals 200
  expect header "content-type" contains "json"
